.page sftp-server 8 the SFTP subsystem of sshd

# SYNOPSIS
*sftp-server*

# DESCRIPTION
`sftp-server` speaks the SFTP protocol (version 3, the one OpenSSH uses) on its standard input and output. sshd(8) starts it, as the logged-in resident, when a client asks for the `sftp` subsystem; it is not run by hand. With it `sftp`, `scp` (OpenSSH 9 and later carry their files over SFTP) and graphical clients such as WinSCP work against this machine.

Everything it does is done with the rights of the resident, so the file permissions of the system apply: `/etc/shadow` stays unreadable, a file written is owned by the resident. Relative paths start in the home directory.

Supported: open, read, write, close, stat, lstat, fstat, setstat (size, mode, times, owner), opendir, readdir, remove, mkdir, rmdir, rename, realpath, readlink, symlink. No protocol extensions are announced, so a client uses plain operations (a rename onto an existing file replaces it). It ends when the client closes the channel.

# SEE ALSO
sshd(8), accounts(7)
